1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,035
When you were five, summer lasted forever.

2
00:00:03,035 --> 00:00:05,858
Now, a year disappears and you barely notice.

3
00:00:05,858 --> 00:00:08,995
This is not your imagination. Time is speeding up.

4
00:00:08,995 --> 00:00:13,575
And science says it has been accelerating every single year of your life.

5
00:00:14,175 --> 00:00:18,232
In 1877, a philosopher named Paul Janet noticed something odd.

6
00:00:18,232 --> 00:00:21,897
To a five-year-old, one year is 20% of their whole life.

7
00:00:21,897 --> 00:00:24,122
To a fifty-year-old, it's just 2%.

8
00:00:24,122 --> 00:00:26,804
The same year, felt twenty times smaller.

9
00:00:26,804 --> 00:00:31,778
Your brain doesn't measure time in hours and days. It measures it in firsts.

10
00:00:31,778 --> 00:00:34,068
And as you age, firsts become rare.

11
00:00:34,068 --> 00:00:40,350
That's why childhood years feel like continents, and the years after thirty feel like corridors.

12
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:44,005
Think about your last vacation. The week flew by.

13
00:00:44,005 --> 00:00:45,999
But looking back, it feels long.

14
00:00:45,999 --> 00:00:47,683
That's the holiday paradox.

15
00:00:47,683 --> 00:00:51,298
When everything is new, your brain records dense memories.

16
00:00:51,298 --> 00:00:53,979
Familiar weeks get compressed into nothing.

17
00:00:53,979 --> 00:00:55,662
Routine is a memory eraser.

18
00:00:55,662 --> 00:00:58,218
Neuroscientists call it encoding density.

19
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:01,522
Boredom feels slow in the moment, but long in memory.

20
00:01:01,522 --> 00:01:05,075
Routine feels fast in the moment, and vanishes in memory.

21
00:01:05,675 --> 00:01:10,305
There's a deeper layer. Your brain's clock runs on dopamine and attention.

22
00:01:10,305 --> 00:01:13,872
Under stress and busyness, you stop noticing time itself.

23
00:01:13,872 --> 00:01:15,874
Screens fill every empty second.

24
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:18,439
But emptiness is where time used to live.

25
00:01:18,439 --> 00:01:22,256
Think about last Tuesday. Can you remember anything specific?

26
00:01:22,256 --> 00:01:24,446
Probably not. It was never encoded.

27
00:01:24,446 --> 00:01:28,450
A week with zero memorable moments is a week your brain deleted.

28
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:32,044
Here's the hopeful part. You can slow time down.

29
00:01:32,044 --> 00:01:34,102
Not with hacks, but with novelty.

30
00:01:34,102 --> 00:01:37,845
Take a new route. Learn a useless skill. Talk to a stranger.

31
00:01:37,845 --> 00:01:41,650
Your brain doesn't measure adventure. It measures difference.

32
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:46,016
People who collect new experiences perceive longer lives in hindsight.

33
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:47,451
Time isn't speeding up.

34
00:01:47,451 --> 00:01:50,195
Your brain is just running out of new pages.

35
00:01:50,195 --> 00:01:54,125
So write something new this week, and watch the year slow down.
